var e=1,t=`funds-gplp-01`,n=`fund-structures`,r=`GP and LP: Who Is Who and Who Decides`,i=`The two-party structure every private fund uses, what each side controls, and where their interests stop being aligned.`,a=`foundation`,o=[],s=9,c=[`CAIA-L1`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`A pension fund wants exposure to [[buyout|buyouts]] but has no deal team. A buyout firm has a deal team and no capital. The structure that puts them together is a limited partnership, and it has two sides.

The **[[general-partner]] (GP)** is the manager. It sources deals, makes every investment decision, values the portfolio, and runs the fund. It has unlimited liability for the partnership's obligations, which is why in practice the GP is itself a limited company rather than a person.

The **[[limited-partner]] (LP)** is the investor. It provides almost all the capital, has liability limited to its [[commitment]], and — crucially — **has no say in investment decisions.** An LP that involved itself in managing the fund would risk losing its limited liability.

That last point is the structural fact everything else follows from. The LP writes the cheque and gives up control. So all of an LP's influence has to be exercised **before** signing, through the terms in the [[limited-partnership-agreement|partnership agreement]] — because afterwards there is very little they can do.`},{type:`concept`,id:`who-controls-what`,body:`**The GP controls almost everything that determines the outcome.** Worth being explicit about the list, because it is longer than people expect:

- which investments to make, at what price, and with how much leverage
- when to sell, and therefore when the LP gets money back
- when to call capital, and therefore how long the LP's money is outstanding
- **the valuation of everything not yet sold** — which is what the LP's reported performance is based on

**The LP controls two things.** The decision to commit in the first place, and the terms agreed at that point.

That asymmetry is not a scandal; it is the deal. The LP is buying access to a capability it does not have, and delegation is the mechanism. But it explains why the terms matter so much more here than in a public market investment. If you buy a listed share and dislike what management does, you sell. If you commit to a ten-year fund and dislike what the GP does, you wait.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`where-alignment-breaks`,body:`GPs and LPs are described as aligned, and mostly they are: the GP earns most of its money from [[carried-interest]], which requires the fund to perform. That is a genuine alignment and it is stronger than in most of finance.

But **alignment is not identity**, and the gaps are predictable. Learn them as a list, because each one has a term designed to address it.

**Fund size versus returns.** [[management-fee|Management fees]] scale with assets; returns usually do not. A GP raising a fund three times larger earns three times the fees whether or not the larger fund performs. The LP wants the fund sized to the opportunity; the GP has a reason to want it larger.

**Deployment pace.** Fees are often charged on committed capital, so a GP earns whether or not money is working. But an unspent fund also cannot raise the next one — which pushes the other way, toward deploying even when prices are high.

**Timing of exits.** A GP approaching a fundraise has a reason to realise something and show [[dpi|DPI]]. That may or may not be the right moment for the asset.

**Valuation of what is unsold.** The GP marks its own portfolio, and those marks affect its ability to raise the next fund. There is no suggestion of dishonesty in noting that the person being measured is holding the ruler.

**The core insight: every major term in a partnership agreement exists because of one of these gaps.** Read the terms as a list of the ways the two sides' interests diverge, and the document stops being boilerplate.`},{type:`table`,id:`the-terms-map`,caption:`Which term addresses which misalignment`,headers:[`Misalignment`,`The term that addresses it`,`How`],rows:[[`GP earns fees regardless of performance`,`[[carried-interest]] and a [[hurdle-rate]]`,`Most GP economics come from profit share above a minimum return, not from fees.`],[`GP could take carry on early winners then lose later`,`[[clawback]]`,`Obliges the GP to return carry if the fund's final result does not justify it.`],[`GP could charge fees on money not working`,`Fees on invested rather than committed capital, and step-downs`,`Reduces the fee base after the investment period ends.`],[`GP could keep transaction and monitoring fees`,`[[fee-offset]]`,`Requires such fees to be credited against management fees.`],[`GP could invest outside its stated strategy`,`Investment restrictions and concentration limits`,`Caps single positions, geographies, or asset types.`],[`GP has little of its own money at risk`,`GP commitment`,`Requires the GP to invest its own capital alongside LPs.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Fund size drift.** A manager who performed well on $500m raises $3bn. The strategy that worked at the smaller size may not exist at the larger one, and the fees are certain while the returns are not. This is the most common way a good manager becomes a poor investment.

**Key person departure.** The returns came from three people and the agreement names none of them. A **key person provision** — suspending investment activity if named individuals leave — is the protection, and its absence is worth noticing.

**Style drift.** A fund raised to do one thing does another, because that is where the capital could be deployed. Investment restrictions are the defence, and vague ones are no defence.

**No-fault divorce absent.** Without a mechanism for LPs to remove the GP or terminate the investment period by supermajority vote, an LP genuinely has no recourse for ten years.

**Conflicts between funds.** A manager running several funds must allocate opportunities between them. Where those funds have different fee terms or different vintages, the allocation policy matters and should be written down.

**Valuation without challenge.** No independent valuation, no auditor engagement on marks, no third-party pricing for hard assets. The reported performance is then entirely the GP's own assertion.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`terms-are-the-investment`,body:`**The most common LP mistake is diligencing the manager and skimming the document.** Track record, team and strategy get weeks; the partnership agreement gets a lawyer's markup and a summary.

That is the wrong allocation of effort, because the terms are what govern the next ten years and the track record is a description of the last ten. Two commitments to the same manager on different terms are different investments — a difference of a percentage point a year in net outcome is entirely achievable through terms alone, on identical gross performance.

So read the terms as economics rather than as legal formality. What is the fee base, when does it step down, is there a hurdle, is it compounding, is there a [[catch-up|catch-up]], is carry per-deal or whole-fund, and is there a clawback with real security behind it. Every one of those is a number.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Ask how much the GP has committed of its own money, and where it came from.** A large GP commitment funded from prior carry is meaningful alignment. One funded by borrowing against future fees is considerably less so, and the distinction is a fair question.

**Ask what the fund's stated size cap is, and whether they have ever turned capital away.** A manager who has closed a fund below its hard cap has demonstrated something no marketing document can.

**Ask who the key persons are and what happens if they leave.** If the answer is "the agreement does not name anyone", you have learned that the LP is exposed to a departure it cannot respond to.

**Ask how transaction and monitoring fees are treated.** A 100% [[fee-offset]] is the LP-friendly answer. Anything less is the GP keeping fees the LP is indirectly paying.

**Ask about the allocation policy across funds.** Particularly where a manager runs both a flagship fund and separate accounts with different economics.

**Ask what independent input there is into valuations.** Not whether the fund is audited — audits test process, not judgement — but whether anyone outside the GP prices the assets.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`The GP decides everything: investments, timing, exits and the valuation of what is unsold. The LP decides only whether to commit and on what terms.`,`GPs and LPs are genuinely aligned through carry, and the gaps are predictable: fund size, deployment pace, exit timing and self-valuation.`,`Every major term in a partnership agreement exists to address one of those gaps. Read the document as a list of divergences, not as boilerplate.`,`Two commitments to the same manager on different terms are different investments, and the gap can be a percentage point a year.`]}],u=[{id:`funds-gplp-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:1,tags:[`gp-lp`,`structure`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`In a limited partnership fund, what does the limited partner control?`,choices:[`Investment decisions, jointly with the general partner`,`Whether to commit, and the terms agreed before signing`,`The valuation of the portfolio`,`When capital is called and when investments are sold`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`LPs deliberately have no role in investment decisions — an LP that participated in management would risk losing its limited liability, which is the protection it is there for.`,`Correct. All LP influence is exercised before signing, through the terms. Afterwards the GP decides and the LP waits, which is why the document matters as much as the manager.`,`The GP values the portfolio. That is precisely why an LP's reported performance is, for unrealised holdings, the GP's own assertion.`,`Both are GP decisions, and both materially affect the LP's realised return through their effect on IRR.`],explanation:`The structure trades control for access to a capability. Recognising that the LP's only lever is pre-signing explains why terms deserve as much diligence as track record.`},{id:`funds-gplp-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`gp-lp`,`alignment`,`fund-size`],concept:`where-alignment-breaks`,stem:`A manager who performed well running $500m raises a $3bn fund. Why is this the most common way a good manager becomes a poor investment?`,choices:[`Larger funds always charge higher fees`,`Management fees scale with size while the opportunity set often does not, so fee income rises with certainty and returns do not`,`Larger funds are required to diversify more`,`The manager will necessarily lose their key staff`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Fee rates are often slightly lower on larger funds. It is the fee *base* that grows sixfold, not the rate.`,`Correct. A sixfold larger fund earns roughly six times the management fee whatever happens. But the strategy that generated the returns may only exist at the smaller size — the deals may be too small, or the capacity genuinely limited.`,`There is no such requirement, though a larger fund may be forced to move up in deal size, which is a related but different problem.`,`Staff turnover is a separate risk and not an automatic consequence of raising more.`],explanation:`The asymmetry is between a certain increase in fees and an uncertain increase in returns. Which is why a manager who has closed a fund below its hard cap is showing you something genuinely informative about their priorities.`},{id:`funds-gplp-01-q3`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:3,tags:[`gp-lp`,`alignment`,`misconception`],concept:`where-alignment-breaks`,stem:`"The GP earns most of its money from carried interest, so its interests are fully aligned with ours." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — carry creates real alignment, but predictable gaps remain around fund size, deployment pace, exit timing and self-valuation`,`False — carried interest creates no alignment at all`,`True — a profit share means the GP only earns if the LP earns`,`False — because GPs typically earn more from fees than from carry`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. The carry alignment is genuine and stronger than in most of finance. "Fully" is the word that fails: each remaining gap is real, predictable, and has a specific term designed to address it.`,`Overcorrection. Carry is the main reason GP and LP interests point in the same direction at all.`,`This is the claim under test. It is broadly right and it is not complete — a GP can earn substantial fees from a fund that returns capital and no carry.`,`For a successful fund carry usually exceeds fees. The claim is not reliably true and is not the reason the statement fails.`],explanation:`The productive stance is neither trusting alignment nor assuming conflict, but knowing the specific list of divergences — because each one corresponds to a term you can negotiate or at least check.`},{id:`funds-gplp-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`gp-lp`,`fee-offset`,`terms`],concept:`the-terms-map`,stem:`A GP charges portfolio companies transaction and monitoring fees. Why does the LP care how those are treated?`,choices:[`Because such fees are prohibited in most jurisdictions`,`Because the LP indirectly funds them through its ownership of the companies, so without an offset the GP is paid twice`,`Because they reduce the companies' taxable income`,`Because they are counted as part of the LP's commitment`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`They are not prohibited. They are negotiated, which is exactly why the treatment matters.`,`Correct. The LP owns the companies paying the fees, so the money comes out of the LP's own asset. Without a fee offset crediting them against management fees, the GP receives compensation the LP has already funded once.`,`Tax treatment at the company level is a detail and not why the LP cares.`,`Commitments are the LP's capital promise and are unaffected by fees charged at the portfolio company level.`],explanation:`A 100% offset is the LP-friendly standard, and anything less is worth quantifying rather than accepting. It is a good example of a term that looks administrative and is straightforwardly economic.`},{id:`funds-gplp-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`gp-lp`,`valuation`,`conflicts`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`Why is GP self-valuation a structural issue rather than an accusation of dishonesty?`,choices:[`Because GPs are not required to have their funds audited`,`Because unrealised marks affect the GP's ability to raise the next fund, so the party being measured is also holding the ruler`,`Because valuation methods are not standardised at all`,`Because LPs are prohibited from seeing the underlying holdings`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Most institutional funds are audited. Audits test whether a process was followed, not whether the judgements inside it were right.`,`Correct. No dishonesty is needed for a conflict to exist. Where judgement is required and the person exercising it benefits from a favourable answer, the structure itself is the issue — which is why independent valuation input matters.`,`There are recognised valuation frameworks. They still leave substantial room for judgement, which is the actual problem.`,`LPs typically receive holdings-level reporting. Access is not the constraint.`],explanation:`Framing it structurally rather than morally makes it a tractable diligence question: who other than the GP prices these assets, and what happens when they disagree? That has an answer, and "nobody" is a meaningful one.`},{id:`funds-gplp-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`gp-lp`,`key-person`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`A fund's returns are clearly attributable to three named individuals, and the partnership agreement contains no key person provision. What is the practical consequence for an LP?`,choices:[`The LP can redeem if those individuals leave`,`The LP remains committed for the full fund life even if all three depart, with no mechanism to respond`,`The management fee is reduced automatically on a departure`,`The GP is obliged to replace them with equivalent individuals`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`There is no redemption right in a closed-end private fund. That is the point of the structure and the reason protections have to be contractual.`,`Correct. Without a key person provision, departures trigger nothing. The commitment stands, capital can still be called, and the LP invests behind people it did not underwrite.`,`Fee reductions on departure only happen if the agreement provides for them, which by hypothesis it does not.`,`No such obligation exists absent a specific provision, and "equivalent" would be unenforceable in any case.`],explanation:`A key person provision typically suspends the investment period if named individuals leave, giving LPs a decision point. Its absence in a fund whose returns are visibly person-dependent is one of the clearest term-level red flags there is.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
